Memory ranges inside the kernel-managed span that are not usable memory.

The loader may hand the kernel one span that covers more than one run of memory (boot_args physBase/memSize) and lists
the ranges inside it the kernel must not use, a gap between DRAM banks or memory the firmware keeps, in the device tree
property /chosen/darwinboot-memory-excluded: (base, size) pairs of 64-bit values, a pair of size zero being an unused
slot. Without the property nothing changes.

arm_vm_init.c keeps a sorted, page-aligned copy of the list in boot-allocated memory (IOKit frees the flattened device
tree). The physical aperture leaves the ranges unmapped, so memory that is not there cannot be reached by a stray access
or a kernel core dump. The boot allocator (pmap_next_page_hi) steps over them. The enumeration for the linear page array
(pmap_next_page) still reports their pages in place, and pmap_startup marks those entries so that they never reach the
free list. pmap_valid_page() is false for them. max_mem and sane_size leave them out; hw.memsize comes from the loader's
memSizeActual, which carries the usable bytes. The kernel's contiguous boot allocations sit behind the loaded image; the
loader keeps max(span / 64, 32 MiB) there free of excluded ranges and the kernel checks it before its first allocation.

pmap_startup also released every entry of the page array up to its estimate npages. The entries past vm_pages_count are
never initialized, and with the linear array they stand for page numbers at and above avail_end, which is not memory the
kernel owns. The release loop now ends at vm_pages_count, and a boot line reports the three counts.

vm.pages_excluded reports the excluded pages inside the page array; vm.pages leaves them out.

Requires a loader that writes the property (DarwinBoot with /chosen/darwinboot-memory-excluded in the tree).

